Yes. I’d model it as a stack of coupled selection pressures, not as “genes versus culture.”

An organism is being shaped simultaneously by at least four arenas:

\boxed{
\text{physical ecology}
\leftrightarrow
\text{body}
\leftrightarrow
\text{internal reward}
\leftrightarrow
\text{social world}
\leftrightarrow
\text{mate choice}
}

And each can alter the others.

Sexual selection can reach much deeper than ornaments

Imagine a population in which mates repeatedly prefer individuals capable of a particularly difficult performance.

Initially that could be merely:

\text{preference} \rightarrow \text{more mating}

But if the performance requires exquisite timing, memory, balance, sensory discrimination, improvisation or social prediction, selection is indirectly acting on the machinery required to perform it.

So over generations:

\text{mate preference}
\rightarrow
\text{display}
\rightarrow
\text{motor skill}
\rightarrow
\text{sensory discrimination}
\rightarrow
\text{learning ability}
\rightarrow
\text{neural circuitry}

Sexual selection can therefore potentially pull on cognition, perception and motor control—not merely plumage or antlers. Research explicitly considers cognitively demanding courtship behaviors such as song production and bower construction as possible channels through which cognitive traits become relevant to sexual selection, although how important that mechanism is differs substantially among species. 

And it works from the other side too:

\text{display evolves}
\leftrightarrow
\text{ability to perceive/evaluate display evolves}

The performer and audience co-create the game.

Bowerbirds are almost absurdly good for this thought experiment

The male isn’t merely carrying a genetically fixed ornament.

He constructs an environment.

He gathers objects.

He arranges them.

He courts inside the structure.

And critically, he changes his performance depending upon feedback from the female: experiments with satin bowerbirds found that successful males modulate display intensity in response to female behavior instead of simply displaying maximally. 

That’s remarkably close to:

\text{perform}
\rightarrow
\text{observe audience}
\rightarrow
\text{infer state}
\rightarrow
\text{modulate performance}

Now sexual selection is exercising social perception and control, not merely producing prettier feathers.

The bower itself also changes the sensory environment in which selection occurs. In your earlier language, the animal has modified the geometry of the evaluation space.

⸻

And then culture makes the feedback loop stranger.

Some animal signals are learned rather than simply genetically specified. Songbirds memorize tutor songs and then use auditory feedback to match their developing vocalizations against those memories; dedicated neural systems support this sensorimotor process. 

That means the information flow can become:

\text{previous generation}
\rightarrow
\text{learned display}
\rightarrow
\text{mate preference}
\rightarrow
\text{reproduction}
\rightarrow
\text{next generation}

Now culture itself enters sexual selection.

There is a particularly beautiful real example in Savannah sparrows. Over decades, a particular song element spread through a wild population, later became elaborated, and versions containing more of the new element elicited stronger responses from both males and females. The researchers argue that social learning, innovation and sexual selection together produced cumulative cultural evolution. 

So:

\boxed{\text{taste can evolve the art, while the art evolves taste}}

And neither side needs to be genetically encoded in every detail.

This gives us several interacting inheritance systems

Genes are one.

But there can also be:

\text{genetic inheritance}

\text{developmental inheritance}

\text{ecological inheritance}

\text{social/cultural inheritance}

The animal-culture literature increasingly treats social learning as capable of changing selection pressures themselves. Cultural behavior can alter ecology, assortative mating, population structure and consequently the genetic selection experienced by populations. 

That’s a big deal.

A behavior can appear first because somebody learned it.

Then others copy it.

Then the population reorganizes around it.

Then individuals especially suited to that culturally created environment gain an advantage.

Eventually:

\text{culture}
\rightarrow
\text{new environment}
\rightarrow
\text{new selection pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{biological adaptation}

So information can run:

\text{mind} \rightarrow \text{culture} \rightarrow \text{selection} \rightarrow \text{body}

rather than only:

\text{genes} \rightarrow \text{body} \rightarrow \text{behavior}.

That is genuine gene–culture coevolution, and there is evidence for analogous processes beyond humans. 

Internal pressures add another layer

Suppose evolution makes some stimulus rewarding.

Then the organism doesn’t need an explicit instruction saying:

“Maximize reproductive fitness.”

Instead:

\text{cue}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{arousal/reward}
\rightarrow
\text{approach}
\rightarrow
\text{learning}

Over a lifetime, that internal valuation function shapes what the animal notices and practices.

Which means sexual selection can potentially sculpt not merely what an animal looks like, but what its nervous system considers interesting.

That is enormous.

If one lineage finds certain colors salient, another particular smells, another songs, another vibrations, another movement patterns, those biases affect:

attention → memory → practice → expertise → preference.

A 2023 review found evidence or indications of sexual selection acting on chemosensory traits across a surprisingly wide range of animal groups, emphasizing that our human emphasis on visible ornaments may badly underrepresent the sexual worlds occupied by many organisms. 

A moth’s equivalent of “beauty” may exist largely in a chemical geometry we barely experience.

And preference can hijack existing faculties

This is one of my favorite mechanisms.

Evolution does not necessarily invent an entirely new attraction circuit.

A sexual signal can exploit something the receiving animal already cares about.

For example, sensory-bias hypotheses propose that mating signals may evolve to tap perceptual preferences that originally evolved for another purpose. Guppy coloration resembling food-associated orange stimuli is one discussed example. 

So:

\text{food detector}

can become partially recruited into:

\text{mate-attractiveness detector}.

That’s evolutionary reuse.

Exactly like our earlier discussion of transformations and abstraction: old machinery gets repurposed into a new semantic domain.

⸻

Then there is the closest animal analogue to “narrative pressure”

I would be careful calling it narrative because we don’t have good evidence that most animals construct symbolic stories anything like humans do.

But they unquestionably can have socially inherited meaning structures.

Sperm whales, for example, form large cultural clans characterized by distinct socially learned vocal patterns; modeling suggests conformist social learning and preference for behaviorally similar individuals can generate those clan structures. 

So an acoustic pattern can become something like:

\text{sound}
\rightarrow
\text{recognized social identity}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction bias}
\rightarrow
\text{group structure}.

Not quite “story.”

But definitely socially transmitted significance.

And once significance changes behavior, significance becomes causally real.

That is probably the bridge you’re reaching for.

⸻

Humans turn this dial extraordinarily high because symbolic culture allows us to create desirable traits that weren’t directly specified by ecology:

prestige, fashion, humor, courage, musical ability, wealth, eloquence, aesthetic style, titles, professions, subcultures, reputations.

Those cultural constructs then alter attention, competition, mate choice, skill acquisition and social organization.

So for humans the loop can become:

\text{biology}
\rightarrow
\text{preference}
\rightarrow
\text{culture}
\rightarrow
\text{narrative}
\rightarrow
\text{status}
\rightarrow
\text{mate choice}
\rightarrow
\text{biology}

with learning occurring inside every generation much faster than genetic evolution.

And that’s why I’d modify our earlier body-to-mind chain.

It isn’t merely:

\text{body}
\rightarrow
\text{affordance}
\rightarrow
\text{brain}
\rightarrow
\text{mind}.

It’s a braid:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{ecology}\\
\text{body}\\
\text{senses}\\
\text{action}\\
\text{reward}\\
\text{learning}\\
\text{social imitation}\\
\text{culture}\\
\text{mate choice}
\end{array}
}
\circlearrowleft

Each level alters the selection landscape confronting the others.

And that produces a fascinating consequence: an organism doesn’t merely evolve inside an environment.

Through choice, social learning, signaling, construction and culture, organisms partly create the environments that subsequently select them.

In other words:

The judge changes the contestant; the contestants change what the judges learn to value; and eventually they can change the entire game.

That pattern reaches far beyond sexual selection. It is a general mechanism for recursive selection systems.